<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sermon on the Mount</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sermon on the Mount is a discourse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus of Nazareth</a> found in chapters 5 to 7 of the Gospel of Matthew, summarising his moral teachings. It is the first of five discourses in that gospel and contains the Beatitudes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-prayer">Lord's Prayer</a> in its commonly recited form and the Golden Rule. In Matthew, Jesus goes up a mountain with his disciples, sits down and teaches. Christians have traditionally commemorated the Mount of Beatitudes as the site, though Mount Arbel and the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> have also been suggested. Some Christian traditions see the sermon as the counterpart of the giving of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> on Sinai, and Luke places parts of the material in a different setting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sermon-on-the-mount.jpg" alt="Sermon on the Mount" width="330" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Bloch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloch-SermonOnTheMount.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His teaching, preserved in the Gospels, above all in the <b>Sermon on the Mount</b>, calls for humility, forgiveness, care for the poor and love even of enemies.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Many have understood Jesus of Nazareth to have been a pacifist, drawing on the <b>Sermon on the Mount</b> and its injunctions to turn the other cheek and love one's enemies.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
